WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.475
A decision template: should we switch models?

2
00:00:02.755 --> 00:00:06.381
Write this down, in this order, and keep it with the eval results.

3
00:00:06.661 --> 00:00:08.027
It fits on one page.

4
00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:09.053
Trigger.

5
00:00:09.333 --> 00:00:10.165
Why now?

6
00:00:10.445 --> 00:00:15.949
A new release, a price change, a quality problem, a deprecation notice, a contract renewal.

7
00:00:16.229 --> 00:00:18.491
Decision rule, written before results.

8
00:00:18.771 --> 00:00:34.280
For example: switch if the candidate’s paired difference against the incumbent has a 95 percent lower bound above minus 1 point, its cost per success is at least 25 percent lower, and its p95 latency stays under 6 seconds.

9
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:40.875
Or: switch if quality improves by at least 3 points with a lower bound above zero, at no more than 1.

10
00:00:41.155 --> 00:00:42.605
5 times the cost.

11
00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:44.421
Shortlist and constraints.

12
00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:52.616
Which configurations, from which providers (Claude, GPT, Gemini, Grok, open-weight), and what was excluded and why.

13
00:00:52.896 --> 00:00:53.813
Protocol.

14
00:00:54.093 --> 00:01:06.211
Dataset version and size, dev and test split, tuning budget per candidate, effort levels, harness settings, trials per question, and the power of the test (the smallest difference it can detect).

15
00:01:06.491 --> 00:01:07.365
Results.

16
00:01:07.645 --> 00:01:19.208
For each configuration: pass rate with interval, paired difference with interval and McNemar p-value against the incumbent, cost per success, p95 latency, failure types.

17
00:01:19.488 --> 00:01:20.640
The frontier plot.

18
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:22.563
Migration cost and risk.

19
00:01:22.843 --> 00:01:33.275
Prompt and test changes, structured-output and tool differences, security and legal review, monitoring changes, vendor concentration, and the deprecation timeline.

20
00:01:33.555 --> 00:01:34.984
Decision and rollout.

21
00:01:35.264 --> 00:01:44.053
The rule’s verdict, any judgment call made on top of it and why, a canary or shadow period with a live metric to watch, and the rollback trigger.

22
00:01:44.333 --> 00:01:45.720
Re-evaluation date.

23
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.413
When you’ll run the suite again, because the answer has a shelf life.

24
00:01:49.693 --> 00:01:52.253
Two outcomes are common and both are fine.

25
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:58.933
“No detectable difference” is a result: keep the incumbent, or take the cheaper option if the rule says so.

26
00:01:59.213 --> 00:02:05.037
“The incumbent’s smaller tier wins” happens often enough to be worth the extra row in every bake-off.

27
00:02:05.317 --> 00:02:11.291
What’s not fine is a decision with no rule, no intervals, and a launch-day screenshot as evidence.
